\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Joint integrals on the circle and three-sphere}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use a finite graph with the based face walks of the
\vocabk{orbit gauge–Higgs weight}{fdip-010A}. Take either #{U(1)}
acting on the unit circle by multiplication, or #{SU(2)} acting
on the unit sphere in #{\mathbb C^2} by its defining action.
Use the real part of the standard Hermitian inner product. Supply
a normalized bi-invariant Haar probability #{\mu_G} as in the
\vocabk{joint gauge and real-matter measure}{fdip-0076}. Fix a
reference unit vector #{\phi^\circ} and give the orbit the pushforward
#{\mu_{\mathcal O}} by #{g\mapsto g\phi^\circ}. Use the product
probabilities #{\mu_E=\mu_G^{\otimes E}} for links and
#{\mu_X=\mu_{\mathcal O}^{\otimes X}} for sites.}

\p{For continuous real face, edge and site factors with the same
invariances as in the finite model, the joint density #{\Xi} and
its product reference measure are invariant under each vertex
gauge. The two marginal densities are
##{\begin{aligned}
 w_{\rm link}(U)&=\int\Xi(U,\phi)\,\extd\mu_X(\phi),\\
 w_{\rm site}(\phi)&=\int\Xi(U,\phi)\,\extd\mu_E(U).
\end{aligned}}
Both are invariant under their respective gauge actions and both
integrate to the same partition integral #{Z}. If #{\Xi\ge0} and
#{Z>0}, then #{\Xi /Z} is a probability density. The conditional
scalar density is #{\Xi(U,\cdot)/w_{\rm link}(U)} at each #{U}
with positive marginal. The conditional link density at #{\phi}
is obtained in the other order under the corresponding positivity
condition.}

\p{Circle multiplication identifies #{U(1)} with the orbit of
#{1}. For #{SU(2)}, choose an orthonormal basis whose first vector
is #{\phi^\circ}. A unit column #{(a,b)} in this basis is the
first column of a unique special unitary operator. Its second
column is #{(-\bar b,\bar a)}. The columns are orthonormal and
the determinant is #{|a|^2+|b|^2=1}. Any other unit orthogonal
column differs by a scalar of modulus one, which the determinant
fixes. This inverse is continuous. Thus each orbit is the whole
specified sphere, and left translation of Haar proves invariance
of the orbit probability. The two actions preserve the displayed
real pairing.}

\p{For fixed vertex gauges the link change at each edge is a left
translation followed by a right translation. The two-sided Haar
change of variables of \ref{fdip-00MR} preserves its measure, and
the scalar change at each site preserves #{\mu_{\mathcal O}}.
The finite product is therefore preserved. Endpoint cancellation
proves invariance of the density exactly as in \ref{fdip-010C}.
Changing the scalar variable in the first marginal, or the link
variable in the second, proves their gauge invariance. Continuous
factors on the compact product are bounded, so product integration
in either order gives #{Z}. In the nonnegative case each stated
conditional density integrates to one. Multiplication by its
marginal recovers the joint density. Backgrounds of zero marginal
contribute zero to the integral of a bounded measurable observable. This proves
the conditional formula for the normalized probability law.}
